{"authored" [{"question" "q-89872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8653792ef8fcb9bd5bc153cb0030a6f37488ef2159a4c40bfbf014d7bab3edd" "basis" 1390 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8653792ef8fcb9bd5bc153cb0030a6f37488ef2159a4c40bfbf014d7bab3edd" "basis" 1390 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/edb-import/verification.json"} {